<p>Recovery from a beard transplant runs on two clocks. The visible one is short: small crusts, redness and some swelling sit on the face for roughly the first week and fade over the week or two after. The growth clock is long: the transplanted hairs commonly shed between the second and fourth weeks, new growth usually starts around three to four months, and the result is fairly judged at about ten to twelve months.</p>

<p>The question almost everyone asks first is when they can shave. The honest answer is that trimming is usually cleared before a razor is, and both are cleared by your doctor at a review, once the grafts have been examined, rather than on a day fixed beforehand. What follows explains why, and what each stage in between usually looks like.</p>

<h2 id="why-facial-skin-heals-differently">Why facial skin heals differently from the scalp</h2>

<p>The face has a generous blood supply, which generally helps grafts settle, and it swells more visibly than the scalp does. The skin of the cheeks and upper lip is also thinner and far more mobile: it moves every time you eat, talk, smile or sleep on one side. Scalp skin over the crown barely moves at all.</p>

<p>Three consequences follow. Swelling and redness are more noticeable, and in the lower face they can drift down towards the jaw and neck. Movement puts mild, repeated stress on fresh grafts, which is why the first days involve eating and talking normally but gently. And the face cannot be covered: a scalp patient can eventually wear a loose cap, whereas a beard patient's recovery is on display from the first morning. Beard skin is also more prone than the scalp to inflamed follicles as new hairs push through, which matters from around the second month.</p>

<p>The principles are the same ones set out in <a href="/hair-transplant-aftercare">our hair transplant aftercare instructions</a>; the stages below are how they typically play out on a face. For what the procedure involves and who it suits, see the <a href="/beard-transplant-gurgaon">beard transplant page for our Gurgaon clinic</a>.</p>

<h2 id="the-first-week">The first week: crusting, redness and swelling</h2>

<p><strong>In the first week, a small crust forms around each graft, the beard area is pink and slightly raised, and some facial swelling is normal.</strong> The grafts are anchored but not yet secure, so the realistic way to lose one is mechanical: rubbing, picking, scratching or a razor. Handling, not infection, is the main risk in these days.</p>

<h3>Days one to three</h3>

<p>The treated area looks speckled with tiny dark crusts, and tightness and mild soreness are common as the local anaesthetic wears off. Swelling often builds over the second and third days rather than on the day itself, and it can spread below the treated zone before settling on its own. The donor area on the back of the scalp will be tender too, and after <a href="/fue-hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">FUE extraction</a> it is covered in small healing punctures that need the same no-scratching discipline as the face.</p>

<h3>Days four to seven</h3>

<p>Swelling eases and the crusts begin to loosen. Itching is common and is the hardest instruction to follow. Crusts should be left to lift with the washing routine you are shown, not helped off with a fingernail, because a crust pulled away early can take the graft with it.</p>

<h3>Sleeping, eating and talking</h3>

<p>Patients are usually asked to sleep on their back with the head raised for the first nights, so the cheek is not pressed into a pillow and swelling can drain. Eating and talking are fine. If the moustache or chin was treated, pat the mouth dry after eating rather than wiping it, and go easy on very wide mouth movements for a few days. Facials, face massages and anything applied to the beard area that has not been prescribed wait until you are cleared. The fortnight in more detail, written for scalp patients but overlapping heavily, is in <a href="/blog/first-14-days-after-a-hair-transplant">the first 14 days after a hair transplant</a>.</p>

<h2 id="washing-and-touching">Washing, touching and what to leave alone</h2>

<p>The first wash of the beard area happens when, and how, you are instructed. It is usually demonstrated rather than described, because technique matters more than product: water dabbed or poured gently, no rubbing, and the area patted rather than towelled dry. Once washing starts, it is what lifts the crusts safely over the following days.</p>

<p>Until your doctor clears each of them, leave alone:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Picking, scratching or squeezing anything in the beard area.</li>
  <li>Razors, trimmers, waxing, threading and hair-removal creams near treated zones.</li>
  <li>Beard oils, balms, aftershave and fragranced skincare on healing skin.</li>
  <li>Steam rooms, saunas and exercise that makes you sweat heavily.</li>
  <li>Long stretches of direct sun on the treated area.</li>
</ul>

<p>Numbness or altered sensation in the beard area or the donor scalp is common and usually resolves over weeks to months. Expected effects like this, and the uncommon complications that are genuinely different, are separated in <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-side-effects-and-risks">the guide to hair transplant side effects and risks</a>.</p>

<h2 id="the-first-trim-and-the-first-shave">The first trim and the first razor shave</h2>

<p><strong>Trimming is usually cleared before shaving with a razor, and both are cleared by your doctor at a review rather than on a date set beforehand.</strong> The decision depends on how the grafts and the skin have healed. Shaving too early can disturb grafts that are not yet secure, and it irritates skin that is still settling.</p>

<h3>Why trimming comes first</h3>

<p>Scissors or a guarded trimmer cut the hair without pressing a blade flat across the skin. A razor does both: it drags across the surface, catches on any remaining crust, and on newly healed skin it is the likeliest thing to cause irritation and nicks. Cutting hair right at the skin also leaves sharp tips that can curve back in, which is how ingrown hairs start. So the usual sequence is a careful trim of the surrounding beard first, and a razor over the grafted area later.</p>

<h3>Why a fixed day is the wrong answer</h3>

<p>A precise day for the first shave is sometimes quoted online. The difficulty is that healing varies between people, between zones on the same face, and with how the first week went. A moustache that stayed swollen for longer, or a cheek where folliculitis is starting, should not be shaved on the same schedule as skin that settled quickly. Your doctor looks at the area and clears it. If your job or an event requires you to be clean-shaven, raise it at the consultation so the date of surgery is chosen with that in mind, rather than asking for an early shave afterwards.</p>

<p>When you are cleared, keep the first shaves gentle: a sharp blade, strokes with the direction of growth rather than against it, no repeated passes and no stretching the skin tight. A trimmer set just above the skin is often easier on a newly grafted beard than a blade. Once healed, transplanted hairs can be shaved like any others; a razor removes the shaft, not the follicle.</p>

<h2 id="heat-helmets-and-masks-in-gurgaon">Heat, sweat, helmets and masks in Gurgaon</h2>

<p>From late April into June, and again through the humid monsoon weeks, the first week on a face is harder here than a standard aftercare sheet assumes. Sweat does not dislodge grafts, but it softens crusts early, makes the area itch, and invites exactly the wiping that does cause harm. Salt on raw skin also stings, which is when people start improvising remedies.</p>

<p>Two-wheelers are the bigger issue. A full-face helmet's cheek pads press directly on grafted cheeks and jaw, and the chin strap runs across the lower beard. Helmets are legally required in Haryana, so riding without one is not the workaround; not riding is. Plan cabs, a lift or leave for the first stretch, and ask at your review when a helmet is reasonable for your own case. A pollution mask or a cloth tied over the moustache and cheeks causes the same problem on a smaller scale, trapping heat and rubbing across the grafts, so keep indoors with the air conditioning on rather than masking a fresh graft.</p>

<p>The wider local picture, including which months make recovery easier to live through, is in <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-recovery-in-gurgaon">recovering from a hair transplant in Gurgaon</a>. It was written with scalp patients in mind, but heat, dust and helmets affect a beard more directly, because nothing sits between them and the grafts.</p>

<h2 id="shedding-at-weeks-two-to-four">Shedding at weeks two to four</h2>

<p><strong>Between roughly the second and fourth weeks, most transplanted hairs fall out.</strong> The follicles stay in the skin and rest before growing again. It looks like failure and is not. For the following two to three months the beard area can look much as it did before surgery, and sometimes patchier.</p>

<p>Hairs often come away with the last of the crusts, which is part of why patients fear they have lost grafts. A hair shed weeks after surgery with a small crust at its base is the expected pattern; the time grafts are genuinely vulnerable is the first days, when handling is the risk. Native beard hair near the grafted zone can also shed temporarily, sometimes called shock loss, and generally returns. <a href="/blog/shedding-and-shock-loss-after-hair-transplant">What is normal in shedding and shock loss, and what is not</a>, covers the distinction in more detail.</p>

<p>This is also the stage when people start comparing their face with photographs online, which rarely helps. The quiet months are part of the process, not a sign that it has stalled.</p>

