<h2 id="regrowth-and-the-result">Regrowth from three months, and the result at a year</h2>

<p><strong>New growth usually begins around three to four months after surgery, often unevenly and finer than it will become.</strong> Density and texture build through the middle months, and a fuller picture is usually visible at around ten to twelve months. Judging the result before then tends to mislead in both directions.</p>

<p>The first hairs to appear are often thin and may look lighter. Some zones start before others, and a moustache or chin can move ahead of the cheeks or lag behind them. Over the following months the hairs coarsen within the range of the scalp hair they came from; they do not become beard hair, as explained in <a href="/blog/beard-transplant-what-to-expect">what scalp hair does and does not do on your face</a>.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Around months three to four:</strong> fine new hairs begin to break through, patchily.</li>
  <li><strong>Through the middle months:</strong> coverage fills in and the hairs thicken, and the area starts to read as beard rather than scattered stubble.</li>
  <li><strong>Around months ten to twelve:</strong> a fair point to assess density, symmetry and blend, and to decide whether any zone needs a top-up.</li>
</ol>

<p>Comparisons are only honest in consistent light, at the same angle and the same beard length, which is why progress is photographed at fixed reviews rather than judged in a bathroom mirror. <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">How lighting and angle change what a photograph shows</a> is worth reading before you compare your own, and <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-growth-timeline-month-by-month">the month-by-month growth timeline</a> explains why regrowth should be judged against fixed intervals.</p>

<p>If a zone is thinner at a year than was planned, a small second session may be discussed. Where staging was likely from the start, it should already have been stated and costed in the original estimate, which is part of <a href="/beard-transplant-cost-gurgaon">how beard transplant cost is worked out</a>.</p>

<h2 id="folliculitis-ingrown-hairs-and-when-to-call">Folliculitis, ingrown hairs and when to call</h2>

<p>As new hairs push up through facial skin, usually from the second or third month, small red bumps or pustules around individual hairs are common. Some are simple irritation; some are infected. DermNet describes <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/pseudofolliculitis-barbae" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">pseudofolliculitis barbae</a>, the inflammation caused when curly hairs re-enter the skin, as closely tied to close shaving, which is one more reason not to rush the razor.</p>

<p>Most of these spots settle with simple measures or a short course of treatment. What makes them worse is squeezing, because a follicle damaged by picking can scar or stop producing hair. Show them to the clinic instead of treating them at home.</p>

<p>Contact the clinic promptly if you notice:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Redness that is spreading, or swelling that increases after the first few days instead of settling.</li>
  <li>Increasing pain, warmth, pus or discharge.</li>
  <li>A fever, or feeling generally unwell.</li>
  <li>Bleeding that does not stop with gentle pressure.</li>
  <li>A painful, growing lump at a graft site or in the donor area.</li>
</ul>

<p>Routine questions, such as itching, a stubborn crust or whether a particular trimmer is allowed, do not need to wait for a review, and many are answered on our <a href="/faqs">frequently asked questions page</a>. Recovery is reviewed by a registered medical practitioner at the clinic, and the timings above are general patient education rather than a promise for any one face.</p>

